.TH YES 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
yes \- output a line again and again
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yes
.RI [ string ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yes
writes its arguments, joined by spaces, as a line, again and again, until
a write fails (for instance when the reader of its pipe is gone, which
also sends it SIGPIPE). With no arguments the line is
.BR y .
.PP
Lines are written many at a time, about 4096 bytes in each write, as GNU
yes does.
.SH EXIT STATUS
1 when a write fails (it never ends on its own).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
yes | head \-n 3
yes no | head \-n 2
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR head (1),
.BR sh (1)
